"bd comments add homelab-3dp \"2026-09-05: four router-updated A records left out of tofu via UNMANAGED_RECORDS in scripts/cf-module-gen.nu (noted as comments in the zone maps): cipher.cc ip.internal, swensons.space ip.internal, nakedelectrons.com ip.boson (target of the proxied singularity CNAME) and ip.eon. Managing them would revert the router's DDNS update on every apply. Import count drops 322 -> 318. Stale _acme-challenge.stats.home.cipher.cc TXT is imported and tracked for deletion in homelab-boq.\" >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"bead updated\""
"bd comments add homelab-3dp \"APPLIED 2026-09-05: 317 resources imported into Garage state with the read-only token; cloudflare-imports.tf deleted; confirmation plan: 'No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.' Phase 1 complete. Next: edit token into sops, then homelab-boq + homelab-qlo + homelab-7d5 as the first real change; phase 2 (Zero Trust, notification policies, web analytics) still to do.\" >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"bead updated\"; jj status 2>&1 | head -40"
"bd comments add homelab-3dp \"Phase 1 built 2026-09-05, zero-diff plan achieved: 322 to import, 0 add/change/destroy (.terraform/cf.tfplan). Not yet applied; waiting on go-ahead.\n\nModule: cloudflare/ (33 generated files + deps/variables/outputs) from scripts/cf-module-gen.nu; root cloudflare-imports.tf (322 import blocks, delete after apply); provider 5.24.0 enabled in versions.tf, cloudflare_api_token variable defaults to null so \\$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN from .env works for plan and the import-only apply.\n\nIn: 15 zones, 94 DNS records (38 Cloudflare-owned excluded), DNSSEC on 13 zones, 12 curated settings x 15 zones, 5 rulesets, Email Routing on 6 zones (catch-all + rules + address), 2 orphan workers custom domains (cipher.one, backgrounds.jakes.dev).\nOut: bot_management (provider re-plans unset optionals as unknown even under ignore_changes), email_routing_settings (provider read bug: support_subaddress), DNSSEC pending zones (homelab-8cm), legacy filters (shadow copies of rulesets, broken generate output), workers/pages/KV/D1/R2/queue (wrangler + legal-docs infra own them).\n\nProvider quirks worked around: catch-all import id is the rule id in cf-terraforming output but zone id in the provider; unnamed rules need name = \\\"\\\" explicitly; tag/created/modified/verified need ignore_changes; workers_custom_domain.environment deprecated.\n\nPhase 2 (Zero Trust, notification policies, web analytics) not started.\" 2>&1 | tail -1; echo \"bead updated\""
"bd comments add homelab-3dp \"Reshaped 2026-09-05 before first apply to the dns/ module pattern: one locals map per zone (cloudflare/zone-<slug>.tf) and one 'this' resource per type with path-like keys (zone_setting.this[\\\"cipher.cc/ssl\\\"], dns_record.this[\\\"cipher.cc/apex/MX\\\"], email_routing_rule.this[\\\"jakes.dev/sf.work\\\"], workers_custom_domain.this[\\\"cipher.one\\\"]); rulesets stay explicit (heterogeneous nested rule lists). Email rules generalized to forward|worker actions (3 of 6 route to Workers). Plan under the new addresses: 322 to import, 0 add/change/destroy. Still not applied.\" 2>&1 | tail -1; echo ok"
"bd comments add homelab-7d5 \"Decided 2026-09-05: after the import, move the AnonAddy MX comments into shared.tf (anonaddy_mx10/anonaddy_mx20 get a comment, zones drop their merge(...) wrappers). Today five zones have no comment or a different one, so this is ~10 in-place updates; do it in the same first edit-token apply as the stale ACME delete (homelab-boq) and the SPF quoting normalisation. Also done today, zero-diff: shared.tf locals referenced from all six AnonAddy zones (merge with per-zone comments where they exist); NAS tailnet IP is var.nas_tailnet_ip from homelab.json, shared with nix.\" >/dev/null 2>&1; bd comments add homelab-3dp \"2026-09-05: homelab.json added at the repo root as the single source for NAS addresses. tofu: main.tf jsondecode -> module.dns (nas_lan_ip, nas_tailnet_ip) and module.cloudflare (nas_tailnet_ip); generator writes var.nas_tailnet_ip for records whose content is that IP. nix: homelab.network option in _homelab.nix defaults from the same file; networking.nix and plex.nix use it. just nix-inert: PURE (drvPath identical). Plan: 317 to import, 0 add/change/destroy.\" >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"beads updated\""
"bd create \"Cloudflare phase 2: import Zero Trust, notification policies and Web Analytics into tofu\" -t task -p 3 -d \"Phase 1 (homelab-3dp) adopted zones, DNS, DNSSEC, settings, rulesets, email routing and two Workers domains. The 2026-09-05 sweep (.terraform/cf-discover/out/account/, rerun with just cf-discover) also found account-level state nobody manages yet:\n\nZero Trust: zero_trust_organization, zero_trust_access_application (3), zero_trust_access_identity_provider, zero_trust_access_key_configuration, zero_trust_gateway_settings, zero_trust_dns_location, zero_trust_device_default_profile (+14 local_domain_fallback entries, +certificates), zero_trust_device_custom_profile (2), zero_trust_tunnel_cloudflared_virtual_network. Account: notification_policy (2), web_analytics_site (3).\n\nApproach: same generator (scripts/cf-module-gen.nu), new account.tf / zero-trust.tf sections with 'this' resources keyed by name, cf-module-gen --imports, zero-diff plan, apply. Expect more 5.24.0 provider quirks of the kind hit in phase 1 (computed fields re-planned as unknown; see the generator header for the workarounds). Decide first whether Zero Trust belongs in this repo or with the tailnet ACL work, since device profiles and the tunnel vnet are WARP-side config.\" 2>&1 | rg -e 'Created|homelab-'; bd create \"Put a Cloudflare edit token in sops as cloudflare_api_token\" -t task -p 3 -d \"Prerequisite for the first real Cloudflare change through tofu (homelab-boq, homelab-qlo, homelab-7d5, homelab-cl2). Phase 1 ran entirely on a read-only 'Read all resources' token from a gitignored .env, which is enough for plan and import-only applies but not for updates.\n\nMint: dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens, custom token, scoped to the zones and features tofu manages (Zone: Zone, DNS, Zone Settings, Zone WAF, DNSSEC, Email Routing rules -> Edit; Account: Workers Scripts -> Edit for the two custom domains, Email Routing addresses -> Edit). Store with: just tofu-secrets-set cloudflare_api_token. versions.tf already prefers the variable over \\$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN. Then delete .env and revoke the read-only token, or keep it for plan-only sessions on another machine.\n\nHygiene, given homelab-c0u and homelab-akc: this token never touches Caddy or journald; it lives only in secrets.auto.tfvars.json.enc and the gitignored plaintext copy.\" 2>&1 | rg -e 'Created|homelab-'; bd create \"cloudflare/: re-add bot_management and email_routing_settings when the provider stops mis-planning them\" -t task -p 4 -d \"Both were left out of the module on 2026-09-05 because of cloudflare/cloudflare 5.24.0 provider behaviour, not by design:\n\n- cloudflare_bot_management: every unset optional attribute (sbfm_*, auto_update_model, optimize_wordpress, suppress_session_score, bm_cookie_enabled) is re-planned as 'known after apply' on every run, even under ignore_changes, so the resource is a perpetual in-place update. Three real variants exist across the 15 zones (ai_bots_protection block/disabled, cf_robots_variant off/policy_only, is_robots_txt_managed, and nakedelectrons.com with fight_mode = true), so it is worth managing.\n- cloudflare_email_routing_settings (the per-zone on/off switch): Read fails with 'Struct defines fields not found in object: support_subaddress'.\n\nOn each provider bump: targeted plan against one zone for each (the generator still has bot-management-section; email_routing_settings needs a small for_each over local.email_routing_zones). When clean, wire them back into scripts/cf-module-gen.nu, cf-module-gen --imports, zero-diff plan, apply.\" 2>&1 | rg -e 'Created|homelab-'"
"bd create \"Delete stale _acme-challenge.stats.home.cipher.cc TXT via tofu after the import\" -t task -p 3 -d \"FOUND 2026-09-05 in the cf-discover sweep: cipher.cc has a TXT record _acme-challenge.stats.home.cipher.cc = WQ0vg2XDpHGAhEhBOqdHlBL-4HZjiN6_0KgthnhtDQE (ttl auto). Caddy's DNS-01 flow creates that record during issuance and deletes it afterwards, so one left behind means a renewal was interrupted, most likely the August 2026 token outage (homelab-c0u). It is inert but it is clutter, and it is the kind of record that should never be in the zone permanently.\n\nPlan: it is imported as cloudflare_dns_record.this[\\\"cipher.cc/_acme-challenge.stats.home/TXT\\\"] with the rest (homelab-3dp). Once the import has applied and cloudflare_api_token (edit) is in sops, delete the entry from cloudflare/zone-cipher_cc.tf and apply: tofu removes the record. First real Cloudflare write through tofu, so a good smoke test of the edit token.\" 2>&1 | rg -e 'Created|homelab-'"
"bd create \"cloudflare/: centralize the AnonAddy MX comments in shared.tf after the import\" -t task -p 3 -d \"Decided 2026-09-05, deferred so the first apply (homelab-3dp) stays zero-diff on the read-only token.\n\nChange: give local.anonaddy_mx20 / local.anonaddy_mx10 in cloudflare/shared.tf a comment (e.g. \\\"AnonAddy MX20\\\" / \\\"AnonAddy MX10\\\"), then drop the per-zone merge(local.anonaddy_mxNN, { comment = ... }) wrappers so every zone just uses local.anonaddy_mx. In scripts/cf-module-gen.nu that means adding the comment to SHARED_ITEMS and letting shared-item-match treat a zone's differing comment as 'use the shared one'.\n\nEffect: ~10 in-place updates (comment only) across cipher.cc, cipher.one, swensons.email (no comment today), ke.email (\\\"Addy.io MX 20/10\\\") and jakes.io/work. Needs cloudflare_api_token (edit) in sops. Do it in the same apply as homelab-boq (stale ACME TXT delete) and the SPF-quoting normalisation from homelab-7d5, then rerun cf-discover and cf-module-gen to confirm the generator reproduces the module with no diff.\" 2>&1 | rg -e 'Created|homelab-'"
"bd create \"cloudflare/: extract shared record and ruleset templates after the first apply\" -t task -p 3 -d \"Config-only refactors that keep every address, so they need no moved blocks or state surgery. Do them after homelab-3dp's first apply, one at a time, each gated by 'tofu plan' showing No changes (or exactly the intended update).\n\nMEASURED 2026-09-05 from the sweep:\n1. AnonAddy mail records. Same five records on four zones at the apex (cipher.cc, cipher.one, ke.email, swensons.email): apex/MX mail.anonaddy.me prio 10, apex/MX/2 mail2.anonaddy.me prio 20, dk1._domainkey/CNAME, dk2._domainkey/CNAME, apex/TXT SPF 'v=spf1 include:spf.anonaddy.me -all'. jakes.dev has the same set under the work subdomain; jakes.io has only the MX pair under work. Template idea: zone map gets anonaddy = [\\\"apex\\\"] or [\\\"work\\\"], records.tf expands it and merges with the explicit records. Wrinkles: ke.email stores the SPF content quoted, the others unquoted (Cloudflare returns exactly what was entered), so normalising quoting is a real (harmless) update; jakes.io/work lacks the DKIM CNAMEs and SPF, which is either intentional or a gap to fix.\n2. Geo-block rulesets. cipher.cc, jakeswenson.com and nakedelectrons.com each have one http_request_firewall_custom block rule on country, written three different ways: 'ip.geoip.country ne \\\"US\\\"', 'not ip.geoip.country in {\\\"CA\\\" \\\"US\\\"}', 'http.host eq ... and not ip.src.country in {\\\"US\\\" \\\"UM\\\"}'. ip.geoip.* fields are deprecated by Cloudflare in favour of ip.src.*. Template idea: zone map gets geo_allow = [\\\"US\\\"], rulesets.tf renders the expression. Normalising the expressions is a real update to the WAF rule; verify each zone still blocks as intended.\n3. DMARC is per-zone (different rua report ids, different policies) and does not template.\n\nPrerequisite already done: record name/type derive from the map key, ruleset computed fields (id, last_updated, version) dropped.\" 2>&1 | rg -e 'Created|homelab-'"
"bd create --parent=claude-guard-110 --title=\"Rules 1: s-expression reader and printer\" --description=\"Read rule files into a typed tree: lists in parens, patterns in square brackets, symbols, strings, keywords (:reason), and ; comments. Every node carries line and column. A printer writes the tree back in canonical layout; it is what rules --export uses. Errors name the position. No evaluation, no rule semantics.\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd create --parent=claude-guard-110 --title=\"Rules 2: syntax to typed rule table\" --description=\"Parse rule, deny, ask, warn, :when, :reason, :instead, bracket patterns with the current tokens (* ... -* -... and the three redirect forms), ?var binders, and tool patterns (write ?path) (edit ?path) into typed structs. Every error carries the node position. The pattern matcher from pattern.rs is reused; only the surface syntax changes.\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd create --parent=claude-guard-110 --title=\"Rules 3: conditions with bindings\" --description=\"Predicates ancestor-has?, under?, and, or, not. Checked at load for unbound variables and arity. Evaluated after the match under each binding; the row fires if any binding satisfies the condition, and the record names that binding. Two-valued in this version; the type leaves room for unknown.\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd create --parent=claude-guard-110 --title=\"Rules 4: loader with embedded file and user file\" --description=\"The shipped rules are a file embedded at build time and read by the same loader. An optional user file at ~/.config/claude-guard/rules.scm replaces it entirely when present (proposal, no merging). A file that fails to load is one stderr line and the guard fails open for that call. CLAUDE_GUARD_RULES env override for tests.\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd create --parent=claude-guard-110 --title=\"Rules 5: executable spec in check forms\" --description=\"A spec/ file of (check ...) forms in the language, one per matcher and evaluator behavior, run by nextest. Failures print the form and the position. Rendering into the semantics page comes later; the forms exist from the first version.\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd create --parent=claude-guard-110 --title=\"Rules 6: migrate the Rust table to the shipped file\" --description=\"Write the shipped rules file that encodes RULES from rules.rs, wire the engine to the loaded table, add rules --export, delete the Rust table. Every existing test passes with the same wire output.\" --type=task --priority=1"
"bd create --parent=claude-guard-1ma --title=\"Elaborator 1: core types, getopt clustering, inner split, partition property\" --description=\"elaborate.rs: Declaration {name, options, subcommands, inner}, Declarations registry, Elaborated {parts: Name|Option|Arg, subcommand path, inner view}. OptionGroup keeps the word text and derives flags and value (attached or the next word). Getopt: -fxd clusters when every letter is a declared short flag; a value-taking short flag swallows the rest of its word or the next word; --opt=value; -- ends options; unknown flags stay whole with no derived flags and no value; undeclared commands elaborate as name plus args. Dynamic words are args. flatten() reproduces the input words; a test runs the round-trip over tests/fixtures/commands.txt pulled from the session logs and reviewed by Jake.\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd create --parent=claude-guard-1ma --title=\"Elaborator 2: (command ...) declarations in the rule language and additive loading\" --description=\"syntax for (command name (option \\\"-C\\\" :value) (option \\\"--no-pager\\\") (subcommand stash ...) :inner (command) | (script :from 1 [:when \\\"-c\\\"])). Built-in inner set in rules/commands.scm: sudo env nice nohup timeout xargs ssh bash sh nu python3. Loader merges built-in declarations with ~/.config/claude-guard/commands/*.scm, user wins by name (D27).\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd create --parent=claude-guard-1ma --title=\"Elaborator 3: matcher and engine integration, inner recursion, log record\" --description=\"Patterns match elaborated units: -* one option group, -... option groups, * one unit, ... any units; a literal or binder against an option with a value matches the flag then the value element by element (so [git -C ?dir stash] binds dir). Inner commands and scripts are elaborated, segmented, and matched like top-level calls. The log record carries the elaborated form. The 'flag value is not an option, by design' spec line flips for declared commands and stays for undeclared ones.\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd create --parent=claude-guard-1ma --title=\"Elaborator 4: claude-guard commands and commands add over carapace export\" --description=\"commands: read the session logs, list every command seen with calls, generic-row hits, flagged calls, status (built in / config / undeclared) and carapace availability, sorted by the two signals. commands add <name>...: run carapace <name> export, convert JSON (Type bool|string|stringSlice|stringArray|count, NoOptDefVal, nested Commands with Aliases) to ~/.config/claude-guard/commands/<name>.scm, print what was written; --stdout previews.\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd create --parent=claude-guard-1ma --title=\"Elaborator 5: spec/elaboration.scm and the round-trip check over the log\" --description=\"check forms for clustering, attached values, --opt=value, --, unknown flags, inner command and inner script; a subcommand that runs elaboration over every Bash command in the session logs and reports any word not reproduced by flatten (principle 15, D22).\" --type=task --priority=1 && \\\nbd dep add claude-guard-1ma.2 claude-guard-1ma.1 && bd dep add claude-guard-1ma.3 claude-guard-1ma.2 && bd dep add claude-guard-1ma.4 claude-guard-1ma.2 && bd dep add claude-guard-1ma.5 claude-guard-1ma.3 && bd update claude-guard-1ma.1 --claim"
"bd create --title=\"Command elaborator: per-command option grammar, flag clustering, and inner commands\" --description=\"Jake's proposal 2026-09-06. An elaborator turns a simple command into {cmd, options: [{flag, value?}], args, inner?} using per-command data: which flags take values, subcommands, and for wrappers/script-takers where the inner command is (sudo/env/nice/nohup/timeout/xargs: command after options; ssh: script after the host; bash -c/sh -c/nu -c/python3 -c: script from an option). Data, not code: (command git (option \\\"-C\\\" :value) ...) declarations in the rule language; an extern elaborator over stdio as the escape hatch. Option data is GENERATED from a completion corpus (candidates: carapace-spec YAML with trailing = for value flags, MIT, ~1500 completers; Warp command-signatures JSON, MIT, adapted from Fig; Fig TypeScript specs) into checked-in rules/commands/*.scm; inner declarations are hand-written. Getopt clustering for declared commands: -fxd splits when each letter is a known short flag, a value-taking short flag swallows the rest of its word or the next word, --opt=value splits at =, -- ends options; undeclared commands stay loose. For declared commands -* and -... consume option groups so [git -... stash ...] matches git -C . stash with no pattern change. Supersedes claude-guard-dvd (ssh), claude-guard-3ev (wrappers), claude-guard-0lm (sh -c) once designed. Next: a spike comparing carapace and Warp on git, ssh, sudo, bash; then D22-D25 in docs/design/rule-language/01-decisions.md.\" --type=feature --priority=1"
"bd create --title=\"Digest long strings in observed payloads instead of storing them\" --description=\"PostToolUse stays registered (it is the 'call ran' signal escalation needs) but tool_response and tool_input.content bloat the log: 510 KB in one session. In Record::observed, walk the payload and replace any string over 256 bytes with {bytes, lines}; keep structure and short scalars; no head sample so secrets are not partially kept. Agreed with Jake 2026-09-05.\" --type=task --priority=1 2>&1 | rg Created; rg -n \"pub fn observed|subject: Subject::Raw\\(envelope.payload.clone\\(\\)\\)|^  // --- the store ---|^  fn a_session_start_record_has_no_tool\" src/log.rs"
"bd create --title=\"Log near misses: patterns that match only if dynamic words stand in\" --description=\"D20 in docs/design/rule-language/01-decisions.md. Run each pattern twice: strict, and lenient where a dynamic word may stand in for any token. Lenient-but-not-strict is a near miss. Pass records gain a near_misses list of {rule, pattern, dynamic words}. Serde-additive on schema v1. Whether a near miss speaks to the model (warn or ask) is a separate rule decision. Needs the bindings API from step 2 (claude-guard-110.2).\" --type=feature --priority=2 && bd dep add claude-guard-$(echo) 2>/dev/null; true"
"bd create --title=\"Read freshness predicates: has-read and read-fresh, deny stale writes\" --description=\"Record mtime and size of every file a Read, Write, or Edit touched at PostToolUse. Predicates (has-read <path>) and (read-fresh <path>); the second holds only when recorded mtime and size match the file now. Scope is the project across sessions, bounded by log retention. Target rule: deny writes to an existing file that is not read-fresh, including Bash redirects and tee, with the last read time in the reason. Design: docs/design/rule-language/01-decisions.md D16. Depends on the rule language (claude-guard-110).\" --type=feature --priority=2 && bd dep add claude-guard-$(bd list --status=open --json 2>/dev/null | head -c 0; echo) 2>/dev/null; true"
"bd create --title=\"Rule language: closed s-expression policy language replacing the Rust rule table\" --description=\"Rules become a file, not Rust const data. Jake wants policies, not programs: closed, total, typed at load, s-expression syntax where term patterns and predicates share one syntax. Extension via an escape hatch (libraries/functions/packages, shape open). Predicate scope includes 'tool calling' (meaning to be pinned in the interview). Deliverables: docs/design/rule-language/01-decisions.md (interview), 02-principles.md (baseline principles proposed by Claude, confirmed or amended in the interview), then implementation steps as child beads.\" --type=feature --priority=1"
"bd create --title=\"Sequence patterns and history predicates: rules that span commands\" --description=\"Two scopes, both future. Within one call: a term pattern over more than one command of a compound command (jj describe then jj new on one line; jj abandon with no jj status earlier in the sequence). Across calls: history predicates over the session log, (ran P), (ran P :within D), (last-call P), (ran-since P Q), where ran means PostToolUse arrived. Escalation and the dialog memory could become (denied-this :within D) and (user-denied-this :within D). Conditions attach to a row. Design: docs/design/rule-language/01-decisions.md D17 and principle 9.\" --type=feature --priority=3 && bd dep add claude-guard-$(true) 2>/dev/null; true"
"bd create --title=\"cwd tracking through a command sequence, cwd-known? and unknown-on-match asks\" --description=\"D13 and D14 in docs/design/rule-language/01-decisions.md. The evaluator tracks a cwd per command: known (resolved path) or ambiguous with a reason. cd with a literal target that exists keeps it known whichever separator follows; dynamic word in cd, cd -, pushd, popd, a missing literal target followed by ;, and cd inside an uninspected string make it ambiguous; a cd in a subshell does not leak. (cwd-known?) is a built-in fact; under? and ancestor-has? evaluate against the tracked cwd and come back unknown with the reason when ambiguous. D14: a matched pattern with an unknown condition asks, with the reason as evidence in parentheses; warn rows skip. Executable spec lines for every case (principle 15). Closes claude-guard-9jh as a side effect.\" --type=feature --priority=2 && \\\nbd create --title=\"Extern predicates over stdio, declared by name, with the extern subcommand\" --description=\"D9, D10, D12 in docs/design/rule-language/01-decisions.md. (extern name (exec \\\"prog\\\" args...) :lifetime ...) declarations at the top of the file. In: rule arguments as argv, CLAUDE_GUARD_CWD / SESSION_ID / TOOL / PROTOCOL=1 env, the term as JSON on stdin (published JSON schema). Out: {\\\"holds\\\": bool, \\\"reason\\\": string?}. Non-zero exit, timeout (default 1s, proposal), or unparseable stdout is unknown. Stderr passes through. Evidence appended to the deny text in parentheses and stored in the record. claude-guard extern <name> [args] runs one extension the way the hook would. A file with no extern declarations is closed by inspection.\" --type=feature --priority=2 && \\\nbd create --title=\"Session facts with lifetimes, computed at SessionStart and stored in the log\" --description=\"D8 in docs/design/rule-language/01-decisions.md. (fact name :lifetime session|fresh|<duration> ...). Session facts are computed once by the SessionStart hook and written to the session log as their own record kind, keyed by session and cwd; a later call with a new cwd computes them lazily for that cwd. PreToolUse never forks for a session fact. Open: a fact that fails at SessionStart is retried once on the first PreToolUse, then unknown for the session (recommendation). Folds in claude-guard-h50 session metadata.\" --type=feature --priority=2 && \\\nbd dep add claude-guard-h50 claude-guard-110 2>/dev/null; true"
