"cd /Users/jakes/code/personal/jakes/.homelab\nbd create \"post-migration cleanup: deploy the NAS, then remove runner leftovers on both hosts\" -p 2 \\\n  -d \"Housekeeping left over from the runner migration in commit qmnonyst. Nothing is broken; the repo and the machines just disagree in one place, and there are dead files on both.\n\n1. DEPLOY THE NAS. This is the only item that matters. The commit removes the forgejo_runner_token sops secret and the forgejo-runner-env template, but that has NOT been deployed: /run/secrets/forgejo_runner_token still exists on the NAS. Until a deploy runs, the repo says the secret is gone and the machine still materialises it. The value was already deleted from secrets/nas.yaml, so the file is a leftover of a credential that no longer decrypts to anything useful, and Forgejo 15 removed the registration mechanism it belonged to anyway.\n     deploy .#nas\n     ssh nas 'sudo ls /run/secrets/'   # forgejo_runner_token should be gone\n\n2. NAS: drop the pre-migration runner state. A real job has now succeeded on the declared identity, so the old registration is provably unnecessary.\n     ssh nas 'sudo rm -rf /var/lib/private/gitea-runner/nas.pre-declared'\n     ssh nas 'sudo rm /var/lib/private/gitea-runner/nas/.labels /var/lib/private/gitea-runner/nas/.token-hash'\n   .labels and .token-hash were only ever read by the module's re-registration guard, which no longer runs. Leaving them is harmless but they read as live state to anyone debugging later.\n\n3. LAPTOP: delete the orphaned macos-ci nix profile. It exists only because the ci account could not read /Users/jakes (0700); the launchd plist references store paths directly and the darwin generation is already a GC root, so it holds four generations of a toolchain nothing launches.\n     ls -d /nix/var/nix/profiles/macos-ci*     # currently 4 links plus the symlink\n     sudo nix-env --profile /nix/var/nix/profiles/macos-ci --delete-generations old\n     sudo rm /nix/var/nix/profiles/macos-ci\n\n4. OPTIONAL: once 3 is done those store paths are unrooted, so a GC reclaims them.\n     nix store gc --dry-run\n\nDO 1 FIRST. Steps 2 and 3 delete the last fallbacks, so it is worth having the deploy land cleanly before removing anything.\" \\\n  --context \"Filed 2026-09-05 after the runner migration landed and was verified by a real job. See memories macos-host-runner-sigttin-hang and forgejo-declared-runner-dot-runner-conflict.\" 2>&1 | tail -3"
"cd /Users/jakes/code/personal/jakes/.homelab\nbd remember --key forgejo-declared-runner-dot-runner-conflict \"MIGRATING a Forgejo runner from registration to the declared uuid/secret model: the stale .runner file BLOCKS startup and you must delete it once. Hit on the NAS 2026-09-05.\n\nSYMPTOM, at daemon start, exit 1, restart loop:\n    Error: invalid configuration: server connection conflict between \\\".runner\\\" and config; only one config file can provide server connections\nThe daemon refuses when BOTH a leftover .runner and config server.connections supply a connection. deploy-rs sees the unit fail and rolls the whole generation back, so the error looks like a failed deploy rather than a state problem.\n\nFIX, one time only:\n    sudo rm /var/lib/private/gitea-runner/nas/.runner\nBack the directory up first. Deleting it ARMS the old landmine for as long as the previously-deployed generation is live: that generation still runs the module's register ExecStartPre, so a restart in that window tries to re-register and fails. Delete and deploy back to back.\n\nIT DOES NOT RECUR, verified rather than assumed:\n  - .runner was not recreated after a full stop/start on v13.1.0\n  - jakes-m4x has run declared for weeks and has only uuid and token, no .runner, despite config.yaml setting runner.file\n  - runner.file is documented as 'where to store the REGISTRATION result', and there is no registration any more\nSo do NOT add an auto-clean step to the config. It would be a permanent destructive operation guarding a file that can no longer be created, and it would silently destroy a .runner if anyone deliberately went back to registration.\nEven a rollback cannot recreate it: the old ExecStartPre does 'rm .runner' and THEN registers, and registration fails on Forgejo 15+, so nothing gets written.\n\nLEFTOVERS that are now inert and can be ignored or deleted: .labels and .token-hash (only ever read by the module's re-registration guard), plus services.gitea-actions-runner.instances.<name>.name — in declared mode the SERVER owns the runner name. The NAS config says 'nas-runner' while the daemon reports 'nas-runner-13', the name given in the Forgejo UI.\n\nSee [forgejo-15-runner-registration-removed] for why registration went away.\" 2>&1 | tail -2"
"cd /Users/jakes/code/personal/jakes/.homelab\nbd remember --key macos-host-runner-sigttin-hang \"macOS host-mode CI runner hanging at 'Complete job' is SIGTTIN, not a runner bug. Diagnosed and fixed 2026-09-05 on jakes-m4x.\n\nSYMPTOM: every step completes with a duration, the step list ends at 'Complete job  0s', and the job sits there forever. Stop the daemon and the job immediately goes GREEN. A 'Password:' prompt may also appear in the daemon's terminal.\n\nROOT CAUSE: the daemon ran in the FOREGROUND from a terminal (just runner-start -> sudo -u ci -> forgejo-runner). Job children inherit that controlling terminal but sit OUTSIDE the foreground process group, so the first one to touch the tty gets SIGTTIN from the kernel and is STOPPED. The runner then waits on a child that can never resume. Killing the daemon kills the stopped child, teardown finishes, the result posts, job goes green. That is why it 'completes' on shutdown.\n\nTHE ONE DIAGNOSTIC THAT SETTLES IT, run while the job is stuck:\n    ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,command | rg ' ci '\nSTAT=T means STOPPED, and it is the signature. Real work shows S or R with CPU time climbing. On jakes-m4x the culprit was actions/cache's dist/save.js.\n\nTWO WRONG THEORIES I BURNED TIME ON, do not repeat them:\n  1. 'Orphaned child holds the step's stdout pipe open so the log stream never EOFs, deferring the final UpdateTask.' Plausible, fits 'green on shutdown', WRONG. Ruled out because all steps already reported durations, so the runner had reached teardown.\n  2. 'forgejo-runner v12 bug, upgrade fixes it.' Upgrading v12.13.2 -> v13.1.0 did NOT fix it, despite v13.1.0 shipping 'don't run host-based executor subcommands in a TTY' and v13.0.0 shipping 'run git subprocesses in a process group'. Those help but do not cover a daemon whose own stdout IS a tty.\n\nFIX: run the daemon under launchd. A launchd job has no controlling terminal by construction, so SIGTTIN becomes impossible rather than merely unlikely. macOS has NO setsid (not in PATH, and nixpkgs' darwin util-linux ships no such binary), so launchd is the only native answer. Declared as launchd.daemons.forgejo-runner in hosts/jakes-m4x/default.nix with RunAtLoad=false and KeepAlive=false, so it is registered but idle and starting it stays a deliberate act.\n\nBEWARE THE LOOKALIKE: after the fix a job can still LOOK stuck at the cache step for a minute or two while actions/cache runs 'tar --posix -cf cache.tgz -z'. That is real work and produces no step output. Tell them apart with the same ps: STAT=R plus climbing CPU time is compression, STAT=T is the bug.\n\nSee [forgejo-15-runner-registration-removed] and [forgejo-declared-runner-dot-runner-conflict] for the identity half of this work.\" 2>&1 | tail -2"
"cd /Users/jakes/code/personal/jakes/.homelab\nbd update homelab-3yb --append-notes \"PROGRESS 2026-09-05, verified:\n  step 1 DONE - deployed; forgejo_runner_token gone from /run/secrets, zero failed units\n  step 2 DONE - nas.pre-declared removed, and /var/lib/private/gitea-runner/nas/ is now EMPTY. Worth noting: declared mode keeps no on-disk state at all, so an empty state dir is the correct steady state, not a sign something is missing.\n  step 3 PARTIAL - the macos-ci profile symlink is gone, but /nix/var/nix/profiles/macos-ci-4-link SURVIVES and is still a GC root. Easy to miss because with the profile symlink gone, nix-env --delete-generations has no profile to act on. Finish with:\n      sudo rm /nix/var/nix/profiles/macos-ci-4-link\n\nCORRECTION to this issue's original disk claim: it said 1.8 GiB per generation, which was the CLOSURE size and badly misleading. 'nix store diff-closures macos-ci-base macos-ci-toolchain' shows the real delta is 36.6 KiB, just the launcher wrapping the same tools, because generation 4 was built the same day from the same inputs as the live toolchain. The generations that genuinely held forgejo-runner 12.13.2 had already been deleted before anyone measured. This was decluttering, not space recovery.\n\nBoth NAS services remain active after the cleanup.\" 2>&1 | tail -1"
"cd /Users/jakes/code/personal/jakes/.homelab\nbd update homelab-3yb --append-notes \"REVISION 2026-09-05: step 1 (deploy) is DONE. forgejo_runner_token is gone from /run/secrets on the NAS, zero failed units, gitea-runner-nas and tailcat both active.\n\nSTEP 3 WAS OVERSTATED. Deleting the macos-ci profile is optional tidiness, not cleanup that needs doing, and it was filed without justifying itself. The honest position:\n  - it IS unused: the launchd plist references /nix/store/183xyydb...-macos-ci-base/bin/forgejo-runner directly, and the only repo mentions left are comments\n  - it costs 1.8 GiB of closure per generation across 4 generations, heavily shared with the live toolchain, so the real reclaim is much less than 4x that\n  - BUT it is a working escape hatch. /nix/var/nix/profiles/macos-ci/bin/forgejo-runner-start still runs the old foreground daemon, and since run.sh was deleted from the repo the store copy is the only one left. The catch is that this fallback reintroduces the SIGTTIN hang and pins forgejo-runner 12.13.2, so it is a bridge back to a known-broken state.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: leave the profile alone until several more CI runs have gone through launchd. One verified job is enough to believe the fix, not enough to burn the bridge. Revisit in a week or so.\n\nSteps 2 (NAS leftovers) and 4 (GC) stand as written.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\nbd update homelab-3yb --title \"post-migration cleanup: NAS runner leftovers (deploy done; macos-ci profile deferred)\" -p 3 2>&1 | tail -1"
"cd /Users/jakes/code/personal/jakes/.homelab\nbd update homelab-c86 --append-notes \"DONE 2026-09-05. Added a paths submodule to the nix-env module in chezmoi's dot_config/nu/env.nu and applied it.\n\nVerified in a login shell (nu -l), where the order is now user profile, system profile, default profile:\n  0  /Users/jakes/.nix-profile/bin\n  1  /run/current-system/sw/bin\n  2  /nix/var/nix/profiles/default/bin\nThat order is deliberate and matches nix-darwin's own: a nix profile install must be able to override a system-wide package.\n\nONE NON-OBVIOUS DETAIL worth keeping: the prepend uses 'path expand -n'. Without -n, path expand RESOLVES SYMLINKS, so /run/current-system/sw/bin becomes a /nix/store/...-system-path/bin literal and PATH gets pinned to one generation instead of tracking the current one. The rest of the paths module never hit this because none of its entries are symlinks into a versioned store.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\nbd close homelab-c86 2>&1 | tail -1\nbd update homelab-h66 --append-notes \"DONE 2026-09-05, landed in commit qmnonyst. Both runners are on 13.1.0 via a full nix flake update rather than the decoupled second-nixpkgs-input plan; Jake chose to move everything at once.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY BROKE, versus what was predicted here:\n  - Caddy's withPlugins hash did NOT break. That prediction was wrong; the build reached activation.\n  - tailcat DID break, twice, and neither was in this issue's analysis. Upstream moved --allow onto the serve subcommand (runtime failure, invisible at build time), and its test suite fails in a many-core Linux sandbox with 'text file busy'. Both fixed in the same commit.\n  - The workflow-command removals (set-output, set-env, add-path) were not audited. Jake accepted that risk deliberately, preferring to fix workflows as they surface. See memory forgejo-workflow-commands-removed-v13 for the replacement forms.\n  - The upgrade did NOT fix homelab-yvb on its own, which was the main reason for doing it. That needed launchd; see memory macos-host-runner-sigttin-hang.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\nbd close homelab-h66 2>&1 | tail -1"
"cd /Users/jakes/code/personal/jakes/.homelab\nbd update homelab-ny5 --append-notes \"DONE 2026-09-05, landed in commit qmnonyst and verified by a real job.\n\nThe NAS no longer registers. hosts/nas/modules/forgejo.nix keeps services.gitea-actions-runner for the user, StateDirectory, podman groups and config generation, but mkForces ExecStartPre down to just the podman-socket wait, removing the register step. Identity comes from settings.server.connections with token_url pointing at a sops secret owned by forgejo-runner.\n\nThe destructive path this issue was filed about is gone: nothing deletes .runner any more, and changing labels is now an ordinary config change.\n\nONE MIGRATION GOTCHA, not predicted here and now its own memory: the stale .runner BLOCKED startup. The daemon refuses when both a .runner and config server.connections supply a connection, and deploy-rs rolled the whole generation back, so it presented as a failed deploy rather than a state problem. One manual 'sudo rm .runner' fixed it permanently; declared mode never writes the file. See [forgejo-declared-runner-dot-runner-conflict].\n\nVerified after deploy: nas-runner-13 on v13.1.0, 'declared successfully', poller launched, and .runner did not reappear across a full stop/start.\n\nRemaining leftovers (.labels, .token-hash, nas.pre-declared) are tracked in homelab-3yb.\" 2>&1 | tail -1\nbd close homelab-ny5 2>&1 | tail -1\necho \"=== open runner-related issues now ===\"\nbd list 2>&1 | rg -i 'jakes-m4x|runner|forgejo|cleanup'"
"cd /Users/jakes/code/personal/jakes/.homelab\nbd update homelab-voh --append-notes \"LARGELY DONE 2026-09-05, and it turned out to be the FIX for homelab-yvb rather than tidying. I had argued against launchd on this issue, framing it as autostart versus the foreground Ctrl-C lifecycle. That was a false dichotomy: launchd's value here is that its jobs have NO CONTROLLING TERMINAL, which is precisely what killed the SIGTTIN hang. RunAtLoad=false and KeepAlive=false keep the no-autopilot property intact.\n\nLANDED:\n  - launchd.daemons.forgejo-runner in hosts/jakes-m4x/default.nix, RunAtLoad=false, KeepAlive=false, UserName=ci, EnvironmentVariables carrying the curated PATH (replaces the old env -i incantation, declaratively)\n  - identity moved into config.yaml server.connections, matching the NAS, which is what let run.sh be deleted outright\n  - flake.nix packages.macos-ci-toolchain removed; the darwin generation is the GC root now, so the root-owned macos-ci profile bought nothing. Verified inert: removing it produced a byte-identical darwin store path\n  - just runner-install RETIRED; runner-start rewritten on just's [continue] attribute plus an && runner-stop dependency; runner-stop and runner-status added; runner-connect trimmed to the token only\n  - VERIFIED end to end: a real WinDough job ran through the cache save and completed without the runner being touched\n\nSTILL OPEN, the reason this issue is not closed:\n  users.users.ci is NOT declared in nix. The account still exists only because sysadminctl created it (uid 502). Adopting a live account into nix-darwin is its own risk and deserves a deliberate pass rather than being folded into a bug fix.\n\nALSO WORTH DOING: delete the now-unused /nix/var/nix/profiles/macos-ci profile.\n  sudo nix-env --profile /nix/var/nix/profiles/macos-ci --delete-generations old\" 2>&1 | tail -2\nbd update homelab-voh --title \"jakes-m4x: declare users.users.ci in nix-darwin (launchd daemon already done)\" 2>&1 | tail -1"
"cd /Users/jakes/code/personal/jakes/.homelab\nbd update homelab-yvb --append-notes \"RESOLVED 2026-09-05. Root cause was SIGTTIN, not the log-stream theory this issue opened with and not a runner version bug.\n\nThe daemon ran in the foreground from a terminal, so job children inherited the controlling terminal while sitting outside the foreground process group. The first child to touch the tty was STOPPED by the kernel and never resumed; the runner waited on it forever. Killing the daemon killed the stopped child, teardown finished, and the result posted, which is exactly why the job went green on shutdown.\n\nConfirmed by ps: dist/save.js in STAT=T with zero CPU while the job hung.\n\nUpgrading to v13.1.0 did NOT fix it on its own, despite that release shipping a host-executor TTY fix. The fix was moving the daemon to launchd, which has no controlling terminal by construction. macOS has no setsid, so launchd is the only native option.\n\nVERIFIED: the same cache step that used to stop dead now runs (STAT=S with a live tar child at STAT=R), exits on its own, and the job completes without touching the runner.\n\nFull write-up in memory [macos-host-runner-sigttin-hang], including the lookalike to watch for: actions/cache running tar -czf produces no step output for a minute or two and reads as a hang until you check STAT and CPU time.\" 2>&1 | tail -2\nbd close homelab-yvb 2>&1 | tail -1"
